@startuml
title Promotion, quarantine, and the second-low rule — v4

skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam responseMessageBelowArrow true
autonumber

queue "Cloudflare Queue" as Queue
control "Queue Consumer" as Consumer
database "D1" as D1
control "Recommendation API" as Recommendation

Queue -> Consumer: Deliver the job in promote phase
Consumer -> D1: Acquire the refresh lease
Consumer -> D1: Count staged recordings; read\nlive_generation and pending_low

note over Consumer,D1
  Validated song batches may already have appended new eligible
  recordings to the live shelf. The publication ledger excludes
  this run's additions from the original similarity baseline,
  and includes them in the run's final discovery yield.
end note

alt Staged generation is empty
  Consumer -> D1: Record outcome "empty"; drop the job;\nkeep the live shelf exactly as it is
  note right of Consumer
    An empty rebuild never promotes over a
    live shelf — visitors keep getting tracks.
  end note

else Nothing is live yet, staging nonempty
  Consumer -> D1: Atomic D1 batch: rebuild dense draw slots;\nonly if a final slot still exists, register + flip\nlive_generation and exact counts; else outcome "empty"
  Consumer -> Queue: ACK the final delivery

else Live shelf exists
  alt Staging shares at least half of the live recordings
    Consumer -> D1: Atomically rebuild slots + conditionally promote\n(same final-nonempty batch as above);\nclear pending_low; outcome "promoted"
    Consumer -> Queue: ACK the final delivery
  else First completed miss below the floor
    Consumer -> D1: Record outcome "quarantined";\nset pending_low=1; discard the staged generation;\nkeep the live shelf; drop the job
    note right of Consumer
      Quarantine protects the visitor from a
      suspiciously disjoint rebuild (for example a
      truncated crawl or a shifted library).
    end note
  else pending_low was already set (second completed miss)
    Consumer -> D1: Atomically rebuild slots + conditionally promote\nregardless of similarity, but never if finally empty;\nclear pending_low; outcome "promoted"
    Consumer -> Queue: ACK the final delivery
    note right of Consumer
      The second-low rule: if two consecutive
      rebuilds agree that the shelf changed this
      much, the change is real life, not a glitch.
    end note
  end
end

note over Consumer,D1 #D9EAD3
  Fresh-install bootstrap exception: after liked songs and
  playlist exclusions are frozen, the first nonempty artist
  result is registered as live and may grow during this same
  generation. Final promotion uses INSERT OR IGNORE for that
  already-registered generation. This avoids an empty site
  during a long first crawl. Later rebuilds append only new
  eligible recordings from validated song batches; existing cards
  stay unchanged until final replacement.
end note

== After every promotion ==

Recommendation -> D1: Read the new generation's exact\nartist slot, then exact track slot
D1 --> Recommendation: One eligible representative

note over Consumer,D1 #FFF2CC
  Final replacement is an atomic D1 batch. Earlier validated-batch
  publications are also atomic batches and only add discoveries.
  Replaced generations are not deleted —
  every generation stays registered in
  recording_generations with its recordings, so the old
  snapshot remains stable and auditable.

  Owner Like/Never decisions remain authoritative in their
  separate tables. Their live draw slots are compacted on the
  decision, and every later promotion filters them again inside
  the same transaction as the final nonempty guard and live flip.

  If the rebuild fails systemically at any phase, the
  existing shelf and published additions remain available; pending_low is neither
  cleared nor confirmed, and the failed job row stays
  until the next Sunday 03:00 UTC tick replaces it.
end note

@enduml
